<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Qinzong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1100">1100</a>–<a href="/years/1161">1161</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Zhao Huan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Qinzong was the ninth emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, reigning from 1126 to 1127 after his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-huizong">Huizong</a> abdicated during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> invasion. When Jin armies captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingkang-incident">Jingkang incident</a> of 1127, he and Huizong were carried off to the north with much of the court, ending the Northern Song; he died in captivity.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1127 Jin armies carried off two Song emperors, Huizong and his son <b>Qinzong</b>, to Manchuria, where both died in captivity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…when the Jurchen Jin captured the capital Kaifeng in 1127, carrying off the retired emperor Huizong and his son, the reigning emperor <b>Qinzong</b>, and forcing the Song court to reconstitute itself in the south as the Southern Song (1127–1279), with its capital eventually fixed at Lin'an (modern Hangzhou).</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1127 the Jurchens captured the retired Emperor Huizong and <b>Emperor Qinzong</b> at Kaifeng; Huizong’s son Gaozong refounded the dynasty in the south, and in 1279 the last emperor, a boy of seven, drowned at the Battle of Yamen.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li><li><blockquote>With Jin troops across the Yellow River and in sight of the capital, Bianjing, Emperor Huizong of Song abdicated in favour of his eldest son, <b>Zhao Huan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-18">18 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Jin dynasty (1115-1234) captured Kaifeng and deported the Song emperors Huizong and <b>Qinzong</b> in the Jingkang incident of 1127, the Jurchen wished to leave a friendly regime behind.</blockquote><a href="/state/da-chu">Da Chu</a></li></ul></section>
